In dermoscopy, melanoma appears as an irregular pigmented lesion with asymmetrical shape, uneven dark brown to black pigmentation, atypical network, irregular blotches, streaks, blue-gray areas and regression structures, with variable erythematous or crusted zones.
In dermoscopic images, melanoma often appears as an asymmetric lesion with irregular, scalloped or notched borders, heterogeneous pigmentation including black, brown, blue-gray and white areas, atypical pigment network, irregular streaks, blotches, dots, and regression structures.
In dermoscopy, melanoma often appears asymmetric with irregular, scalloped borders, heterogeneous colors (brown, black, blue-gray, red, white), atypical pigment network, irregular dots/globules, streaks or regression areas; variations include nodular, amelanotic or acral presentations.
In dermoscopy, melanoma often appears as an asymmetrical, irregularly pigmented lesion with uneven borders, multiple colors (black, brown, blue-gray, red), atypical network disruption, irregular dots/globules, streaks, and varied regression structures.
On dermoscopy melanoma often appears asymmetric with irregular, poorly demarcated borders, an atypical pigment network, uneven blotches, streaks and dots, and multiple colors (brown, black, blue-gray, white, red); variations include amelanotic or heavily pigmented lesions.
Dermoscopic melanoma often appears as an asymmetrical lesion with irregular, poorly defined borders, heterogeneous pigmentation—black, brown, blue-gray and white areas—atypical pigment network, irregular dots/globules, streaks or regression structures.
In dermoscopic images, melanoma appears as an irregular, multicolored lesion with asymmetrical borders, atypical pigment network, blue-white veil, irregular dots and globules; variation 7 may show focal regression and chaotic vascular patterns.
On dermoscopy, melanoma often appears as an asymmetric lesion with an irregular, spiculated pigment network, multiple colors (light to dark brown, black, blue-gray, white), atypical dots and globules, streaks or pseudopods, and regression areas.
In dermoscopy melanoma often appears as an asymmetric lesion with variegated colors—brown, black, blue-gray and red—featuring an atypical pigment network, irregular blotches, streaks, regression areas and peripheral dots.
Dermoscopy of melanoma often shows an asymmetric, irregular pigment network with multiple colors—black, brown, blue-gray, white, and red—irregular dots or globules, blotches, streaks, and peripheral pseudopods, with variable size and pattern.
